Two declarations on the parent: `display: flex`, `justify-content: center`, `align-items: center`. That's the whole answer mechanically, but I'd explain *why* it works rather than just recite it.

`justify-content` operates on the **main axis** — by default, horizontal, since `flex-direction` defaults to `row`. `align-items` operates on the **cross axis** — vertical in a row. Set both to `center` and you've centered the child on both axes independently, with no need to know its width or height ahead of time.

```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  min-height: 300px;
}
```

This beats the pre-flexbox techniques — `position: absolute` with `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`, or table-cell hacks — because it doesn't require knowing the child's dimensions, doesn't break if the child resizes, and doesn't fight the rest of the layout. It also keeps working if I flip `flex-direction` to `column`, because the axes and their properties rotate together.

**Testing:** whether you understand the axis model or just memorized a centering snippet.

This is the flexbox bug almost everyone hits once: flex items have an **implicit `min-width: auto`** (or `min-height: auto` for columns), which sets a floor equal to the item's content minimum size — for text, that's usually the width of its longest unbreakable word. `flex-shrink` only distributes space *within* the room the browser is willing to give the item, and it is never allowed to shrink an item below that automatic floor.

The fix is one declaration: `min-width: 0` on the offending item, which removes the auto floor and lets `flex-shrink` actually do its job. I'd usually pair it with `overflow: hidden` and `text-overflow: ellipsis` so the now-genuinely-shrinkable content degrades gracefully instead of just clipping mid-word.

```css
.item {
  min-width: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
```

I bring this up proactively in interviews because it's the single most common "flexbox is broken" bug report in real codebases, and most people debug it for twenty minutes assuming `flex-shrink` itself is malfunctioning.

**Testing:** whether you've actually shipped flexbox layouts or only read about the model.
